## To [[be]] [[typical]] or [[characteristic]] of (someone or something).
# {{lb|en|with a clause}}
## {{lb|en|informal|idiomatic|chiefly|US|MLE|and|internet slang}} To [[say]].
##: {{syn|en|be all|be all like}}
##: {{ux|en|If he’'''s like''' “I don’t want to”, then '''be like''' “Pretty please! – It means a lot to me”.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|year=1995|writer=w:Amy Heckerling|title=w:Clueless|role=Dionne|actor=Stacey Dash|passage=This weekend he called me up and he’s all “Where were you today?” and I’'''m like''' “I’m at my Grandmother’s house” {{...}}}}
## To be [[as though]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=D. E. Lee|chapter=Jimmy Nails|title=The Sky After Rain|location=Omaha, Neb.|publisher=Brighthorse Books|year=2017|page=226|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/skyafterrainnove0000leed/page/226/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-944467-03-6|passage=“People bet on you. Whether you win or lose. How many rounds. Anything. You’re good for the economy. Don’t '''be like''' you don’t know.” / {{...}} Yeah, he knew. Maybe he didn’t care.}}

====Usage notes====
* The use of “be like” to mean “say” is considered by many to be overly informal, and normally only occurs in spoken English.
* The words after the “be like” may not actually be what was said, but instead a summary of what was said. Similarly, unlike “say”, “be like” may be impersonal, e.g. ‘She was really insistent. It was like “I really need that right now!”’ Speakers who most often use “be like” may use “say” to emphasize that the words are a direct quotation, e.g. if the specific wording is relevant.
* The words after the “be like” may not actually be spoken, but instead be intended to represent a mood in which that thing might be said.<!-- http://books.google.com/books?id=joyocJBy4L8C&pg=PA168&dq=%22be+like,+wow%22&lr=&ei=TXf5R72nDIOOywS0sZ1w&client=iceweasel-a&sig=gLULpp_ECZf8PkL1kZfdecv2VTI
-->
* ''Like'' can now also be used without ''be'', as in “She walked in like ‘we’re through!’”; see the entry ''[[like#Particle|like]]''.

